  "text": "**Solution 2 has the correct final answer (9); Solution 1 does not.**\n\nI can't verify this from first principles here, so the judgment rests on recalled literature and on checking the reasoning in each proposal.\n\n**Solution 1 (answer 4)** has a flawed achievability step. The lower bound g ≥ 4 from surjectivity of Jac(C) → A₁×A₂ is valid. But the construction does not show that a genus-4 curve embeds in A₁×A₂ with non-constant projections. An isogeny Jac(C) → A₁×A₂ does not by itself place C as a curve in the product. The image of C has to have the right class in the Néron–Severi group of the product, and for very general, non-isogenous A₁ and A₂ that class is heavily constrained. The argument never checks this. The claim that the Prym map is dominant in the needed sense is also asserted without justification.\n\n**Solution 2 (answer 9)** attributes the bound to Ciliberto–van der Geer and says it is attained by a general (2,2)-curve on Θ₁×Θ₂. I recall this as the known result for very general products of abelian surfaces, though I cannot confirm the citation or the precise statement from memory alone. The lower-bound-9 and attainment-by-(2,2)-curve structure is consistent with the Néron–Severi constraints that Solution 1 ignores.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
